@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Trees & Ensembles — Selection Map (bias, variance, correlation)
' Synthesis of the tree family: why one tree is weak and why averaging fixes it
' Level:  1

actor "Human expert\ntabular, mixed-type features" as H

component "Single decision tree\naxis-aligned splits" as T <<app>>
component "Bagging\naverage over bootstrap samples" as BAG <<async>>
component "Random forest\nbagging + feature subsampling" as RF <<host>>
component "Gradient boosting\nfit each tree to the residual" as GB <<host>>

H ARROW_OPTIONAL T : need an explainable rule set
H ARROW_MAIN RF : strong default,\nlittle tuning
H ARROW_MAIN GB : squeeze out accuracy,\naccept tuning
T ARROW_MAIN BAG : variance is the problem
BAG ARROW_MAIN RF : decorrelate the trees

note bottom of RF
  A single deep tree has **low bias and high
  variance** — it memorizes. Averaging cuts
  variance, but only to the extent the trees are
  //independent//, which is why a random forest also
  subsamples features at each split
  (""random-forests.puml"").
  ----
  **Bagging vs boosting:** bagging fits trees in
  parallel to reduce variance; boosting fits them
  sequentially to reduce bias, each tree correcting
  its predecessors' residuals.
end note
@enduml
